"""Results narrative for the new evidence, retaining subsection and float structure."""
from pathlib import Path
import json,re
import numpy as np
import pandas as pd
WORK=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1];A=WORK/'analysis';P=WORK.parent/'paper_en_partc_cv5'
ORDER=['Panel Logit','SVM','Random Forest','XGBoost','FFNN','DNN','Qwen 2B ZS','Qwen 2B SFT','Qwen 9B ZS','Qwen 9B SFT']
def before_float(s,label,new):
    a=s.index('\\label{'+label+'}')+len('\\label{'+label+'}')
    b=min(p for p in [s.find('\\begin{table}',a),s.find('\\begin{figure}',a)] if p>=0)
    return s[:a]+'\n\n'+new.strip()+'\n\n'+s[b:]
def main():
    metrics=pd.read_csv(A/'prediction_mean_sd.csv').set_index('Model')
    fare=pd.read_csv(A/'direct_fare_summary.csv').set_index('Model')
    vot=pd.read_csv(A/'direct_vot_summary.csv').set_index('Model')
    fits=pd.read_csv(A/'fit_audit.csv');fits=fits[fits.fold.eq(0)&fits.projection.eq('BASE_MNL')].set_index('Model')
    assert set(ORDER).issubset(metrics.index) and set(ORDER).issubset(vot.index)
    def n(model,key,source=metrics):return f'{source.loc[model,key]:.3f}'
    labels={'Panel Logit':'Pooled logit'}
    best={k:labels.get(x,x) for k,x in [('Accuracy',metrics.Accuracy_mean.idxmax()),('Macro-F1',metrics.Macro_F1_mean.idxmax()),('Log Loss',metrics.Log_Loss_mean.idxmin())]}
    acc,macro,ll=best['Accuracy'],best['Macro-F1'],best['Log Loss']
    s=(P/'sections_en/04_results.tex').read_text(encoding='utf-8')
    # The section introduction itself remains concise and retains its approved role.
    s=before_float(s,'sec:results-prediction',rf'''Table~\ref{{tab:tab01}} reports prediction metrics for the common evaluation cohort of 651 respondents and 3,468 cases, with mean and standard deviation calculated across its five fold-specific portions. Each candidate prediction comes from the model assigned to that respondent's held-out fold. AV adoption accounts for 17.6\% of the pooled cohort, so always predicting incumbent-mode retention gives 82.4\% pooled Accuracy. Macro-F1 gives equal weight to adoption and retention, whereas AV-class F1 and recall measure adoption identification directly. A baseline using the other four folds' AV prevalence has pooled Log Loss 0.465.

The model rankings depend on the evaluation criterion. {acc} has the highest mean Accuracy, {macro} the highest mean Macro-F1, and {ll} the lowest mean Log Loss. These criteria answer different questions: overall classification can favor retention in this imbalanced cohort, while Macro-F1 gives the two outcomes equal importance and Log Loss evaluates the assigned probabilities. The fold standard deviations describe variation across evaluated respondent groups and their associated fitted models, rather than uncertainty from repeated training seeds.

For Qwen, SFT increases mean Macro-F1 from {n('Qwen 2B ZS','Macro_F1_mean')} to {n('Qwen 2B SFT','Macro_F1_mean')} for 2B and from {n('Qwen 9B ZS','Macro_F1_mean')} to {n('Qwen 9B SFT','Macro_F1_mean')} for 9B. Mean Log Loss decreases from {n('Qwen 2B ZS','Log_Loss_mean')} to {n('Qwen 2B SFT','Log_Loss_mean')} and from {n('Qwen 9B ZS','Log_Loss_mean')} to {n('Qwen 9B SFT','Log_Loss_mean')}, respectively. The probability-quality gain is therefore much smaller for 9B. Accuracy increases from {n('Qwen 2B ZS','Accuracy_mean')} to {n('Qwen 2B SFT','Accuracy_mean')} for 2B but decreases from {n('Qwen 9B ZS','Accuracy_mean')} to {n('Qwen 9B SFT','Accuracy_mean')} for 9B.

The adoption-specific diagnostics clarify the Macro-F1 changes. Mean AV-class F1 rises from {n('Qwen 2B ZS','AV_F1_mean')} to {n('Qwen 2B SFT','AV_F1_mean')} for 2B and from {n('Qwen 9B ZS','AV_F1_mean')} to {n('Qwen 9B SFT','AV_F1_mean')} for 9B. Mean AV recall increases from {n('Qwen 2B ZS','AV_recall_mean')} to {n('Qwen 2B SFT','AV_recall_mean')} and from {n('Qwen 9B ZS','AV_recall_mean')} to {n('Qwen 9B SFT','AV_recall_mean')}, respectively. SFT identifies more adopters, but the resulting balance also includes more retention errors for 9B. Thus, improved adoption identification does not imply that every classification metric improves.

These results provide a qualified answer to RQ1. Under the same five-fold assignment, choice-based SFT improves adoption identification and balanced adoption--retention performance at both scales. Its effects on Accuracy and probability quality differ by model size. The two SFT systems have similar mean Macro-F1, and their predictive comparison with conventional candidates remains metric-specific. The results do not establish a uniform predictive advantage of the larger LLM or of SFT over every conventional model.''')
    s=before_float(s,'sec:results-basic-response',r'''Figure~\ref{fig:fig02} compares Qwen probability responses to changes in AV fare, IVT, and waiting/access time with the full-survey reference. The upper panels show respondent-balanced mean AV probabilities at each service level; the lower panels show changes from each model's BASE. The lower panels separate the response to an edited attribute from differences in baseline probability levels. Each respondent keeps the same assigned frozen model along every ladder.

Fare and IVT have negative point estimates in H-MNL, but their precision differs. Fare is clearly identified, whereas the IVT coefficient has $p=0.098$ and waiting/access time has $p=0.497$. Over the specified ladders, the reference has larger probability changes for fare than for IVT and relatively small waiting/access changes. This comparison concerns the chosen service edits, not coefficients measured in different units; the fitted IVT and waiting responses remain uncertain human references.

Both Qwen scales already show a negative average fare response in the zero-shot state. The BASE-centered 2B fare curve is close to the reference over several rungs before SFT, despite its higher baseline probabilities. SFT changes its level and shape without producing uniformly smaller differences at every rung. The larger 9B zero-shot response to improving fares becomes more moderate after SFT. Because elasticity also depends on probability and fare levels, the changes in these curves and in mean elasticity are examined separately.

The IVT response changes more visibly. Qwen 9B ZS responds much more strongly than the survey-based point estimate, especially when IVT improves. Its SFT response is substantially flatter and lies closer to the reference curve over the evaluated range. The 2B IVT curve also becomes flatter: the pronounced zero-shot decrease under deteriorating IVT is reduced after training. These are changes relative to an uncertain fitted reference, rather than direct observations of human responses under the edited conditions.

Waiting/access responses are smaller after SFT at both scales, but the curves do not reproduce the reference direction at every rung. At the largest increase in waiting/access time, both SFT systems show a small positive BASE-centered change. Their near-flat responses therefore should not be interpreted as uniformly correct directional behavior. This comparison uses the same physically valid common subset for all waiting/access rungs and models.

Figure~\ref{fig:fig02} consequently shows attribute-specific changes rather than a uniform rise or fall in sensitivity. Some BASE-centered responses become closer to the survey-based point estimates, while probability levels and local response directions remain different. The following analyses examine whether these average patterns also appear in the distributions of fare elasticity and VOT.''')
    ftext=rf'''Table~\ref{{tab:tab02}} reports Direct fare elasticity across all six adjacent fare intervals for each case. The human reference is negative throughout, with mean {n('Human reference','Mean',fare)}. Conventional candidates produce different distributions: Pooled logit and SVM have means {n('Panel Logit','Mean',fare)} and {n('SVM','Mean',fare)}, while Random Forest and XGBoost have means {n('Random Forest','Mean',fare)} and {n('XGBoost','Mean',fare)}. Their negative-interval shares and dispersion show whether those averages arise from consistently declining adoption probabilities or a mixture of nearly flat and more responsive intervals. FFNN and DNN have elasticity standard deviations {n('FFNN','SD',fare)} and {n('DNN','SD',fare)}, respectively, illustrating why a single average is insufficient to compare service responses.

The Qwen scales start on opposite sides of the human mean. Qwen 2B ZS has mean elasticity $-0.335$, while 9B ZS has $-1.343$. After SFT, the means become $-0.485$ and $-0.461$, respectively. The absolute differences from the human arc-elasticity mean decline from 0.495 to 0.346 for 2B and from 0.513 to 0.369 for 9B. Both SFT means are less negative than the reference, so the reduced differences do not imply matching its sensitivity level.

For 9B, dispersion also decreases. The standard deviation falls from 3.723 to 0.815, the median changes from $-1.073$ to $-0.257$, and the negative-interval share increases from 70.46\% to 78.42\%. Its interquartile range changes from $[-2.955,\,0.284]$ to $[-0.680,\,-0.009]$. This places more of the central distribution below zero and removes much of the extreme zero-shot dispersion, although the median remains less negative than the human median.

The 2B distribution changes differently. Its negative-interval share increases from 62.90\% to 67.03\%, but its standard deviation increases from 0.869 to 1.021. The median moves from $-0.302$ to $-0.194$, farther from the human median of $-0.536$. Thus, a smaller difference in the mean coexists with a less favorable median comparison and greater overall dispersion. These outcomes distinguish movement of an average from movement of the whole distribution.

Non-negative responses remain in approximately one-third of the 2B SFT intervals and one-fifth of the 9B SFT intervals. The mean changes therefore do not remove all cases in which a fare increase leaves adoption probability unchanged or raises it. The interval-level distribution preserves these responses rather than averaging them away within each traveler--trip case.'''
    s=before_float(s,'sec:results-elasticity',ftext)
    conventional_signs=', '.join(f"{model}: {vot.loc[model,'Joint conventional-sign (%)']:.1f}\\%" for model in ['SVM','Random Forest','XGBoost','FFNN','DNN'])
    s=before_float(s,'sec:results-vot',rf'''Table~\ref{{tab:tab03}} and Figure~\ref{{fig:fig06}} summarize the time--cost response through aggregate Direct VOT, the case-level VOT distribution, computability, and response-sign consistency. A positive ratio is not sufficient for the usual economic interpretation: both the fare and IVT slopes must be negative. This distinction matters when an apparently moderate VOT results from atypical component responses or when a near-zero fare slope makes the ratio unstable.

The conventional candidates differ in how often this sign condition is satisfied. Among computable cases, the expected-sign percentages are {conventional_signs}. Pooled logit's aggregate Direct VOT is {vot.loc['Panel Logit','Population Direct VOT-eq.']:.3f} kKRW/h, close to the fitted human value, whereas Random Forest gives a negative aggregate ratio of {vot.loc['Random Forest','Population Direct VOT-eq.']:.3f}. Only {vot.loc['FFNN','Computable (%)']:.2f}\% of FFNN cases pass the denominator rule, so its conditional VOT distribution omits a large share of evaluated contexts. Aggregate ratios and signed case distributions must therefore be read with computability and component signs. These different conventional results also prevent a uniform claim of greater behavioral validity for the LLMs.

Qwen ZS has expected-sign rates of 85.20\% for 2B and 92.94\% for 9B. These high proportions coexist with VOT levels above the survey reference. The human aggregate Direct VOT is 6.599 kKRW/h; the zero-shot values are 12.743 for 2B and 27.771 for 9B. The 9B zero-shot distribution is particularly dispersed, with an interquartile range of 11.672--50.599 kKRW/h and a long upper tail.

After SFT, aggregate Direct VOT becomes 5.858 kKRW/h for 2B and 7.577 for 9B. The absolute gaps to the human Direct reference decrease from 6.144 to 0.741 and from 21.172 to 0.978 kKRW/h, respectively. The two SFT values lie on opposite sides of the fitted reference. These are point-estimate comparisons; the human coefficient ratio itself has a broad confidence set including zero.

The central distributions also narrow. The 2B interquartile range changes from 5.726--29.574 to approximately 0.000--12.863 kKRW/h, and its median decreases from 13.988 to 5.881. For 9B, the range changes from 11.672--50.599 to 2.989--13.029, with the median decreasing from 24.308 to 6.899. Both medians move closer to the human Direct median of 6.563, and the upper tails contract. The human distribution is generated by the common-slope fitted reference and is not an observed distribution of individual preferences.

Computability improves only slightly: from 92.96\% to 93.19\% for 2B and from 98.79\% to 99.05\% for 9B. Expected-sign rates instead decrease to 80.91\% and 91.12\%. Consequently, narrower VOT distributions and closer aggregate values do not coincide with better component-sign consistency. This is particularly relevant for 2B, whose central distribution extends to nearly zero and whose computable cases include more unexpected-sign ratios after SFT.

These results separate three changes: the proportion of computable cases, the direction of their component responses, and the level and spread of the resulting ratios. SFT makes Qwen VOT distributions more concentrated and their central values closer to the selected reference, while leaving directional exceptions that require separate evaluation. The evidence does not support describing every aspect of the time--cost relationship as improved.''')
    s=before_float(s,'sec:results-subgroup',r'''Figures~\ref{fig:fig03} and~\ref{fig:fig05} compare fare elasticity and VOT across age, sex, and survey framing. The human curves here come from the separate interaction model fitted to the full retained survey. The purpose is to examine whether changes in aggregate responses are accompanied by changes in observed group contrasts. The boxplots describe fitted response distributions within groups; their spread is not a confidence interval for the human interaction coefficients.

For fare elasticity, the mean difference between the older and younger groups moves toward the fitted human contrast after SFT at both scales, but remains substantially smaller. Group ordering does not improve uniformly. The fitted human reference has a more negative mean response for women than for men, while both Qwen SFT systems show the reverse ordering with small sex differences. Framing contrasts also depend on model state. A smaller overall mean-reference gap therefore cannot be read as consistent recovery of between-group differences.

The contrast is clear for age-specific VOT. The human interaction reference gives aggregate ratios of 2.184 kKRW/h for respondents aged 50 or over and 9.465 for those below 50. Qwen 2B SFT gives nearly identical values across these groups, 5.865 and 5.853, while Qwen 9B SFT gives 7.375 and 7.732. The SFT distributions can therefore become narrower overall while retaining much smaller age differences than this fitted reference. For 2B, even the very small ordering is reversed.

These subgroup comparisons remain exploratory. In the full-survey interaction fit, the IVT-by-age term has $p=0.034$, while the joint fare/IVT age test has $p=0.090$; the joint sex and framing tests have $p=0.075$ and $p=0.446$. The estimated contrasts are therefore not equally well supported. The figures identify where model responses differ from a selected empirical reference, without establishing that those point estimates exhaust human heterogeneity. Aggregate changes and subgroup agreement are assessed separately in the Discussion.''')
    s=before_float(s,'sec:results-correspondence',r'''Tables~\ref{tab:tab05} and~\ref{tab:tab06} apply the same 23-parameter logit specification to all retained human survey choices and to Qwen's pooled out-of-fold BASE-generated choices. They compare conditional associations and their statistical precision. Coefficients from separate logits also depend on utility scale and should not be interpreted as changes in the LLM's internal variable importance. Table~\ref{tab:tab07} and Figures~\ref{fig:fig10} and~\ref{fig:fig11} then compare human-reference alignment and correspondence between the economic representation and the same fold-assigned agents' Direct responses.

Fare is a significant negative determinant in H-MNL and all four Qwen BASE projections. Human IVT has $p=0.098$ and waiting/access time has $p=0.497$, so the precision of their fitted reference relationships is lower. Demographic and incumbent-mode associations also vary across the survey and generated-choice fits, as shown in Table~\ref{tab:tab05}. H-MNL and both SFT GC-MNL VOT confidence sets are bounded but include zero. Their finite point estimates therefore do not establish a precisely identified positive time value.

Table~\ref{tab:tab07} separates the two comparisons. BASE choice-based measures and Direct summaries are each compared with their corresponding human reference. CF-MNL has no observed human counterfactual counterpart and is compared with Direct. These main economic fits pool the outputs assigned across the five folds. They describe the cross-fitted system rather than a single universal adapter, and the scalar gaps supplement the response distributions.''')
    s=before_float(s,'sec:results-elasticity-correspondence',rf'''To assess changes in fare sensitivity after SFT, we compare elasticity estimates inferred from BASE-generated choices with the mean and distribution of arc elasticities calculated under counterfactual fare conditions. The comparison examines movement toward the survey-based references and correspondence with the same assigned agents' responses. Figure~\ref{{fig:fig10}} places the estimates relative to the case-mean arc-elasticity distributions; Table~\ref{{tab:tab07}} reports scalar differences. The boxes retain between-case variation, whereas Table~\ref{{tab:tab02}} retains all six intervals separately.

The conventional models provide context for this comparison. Their mean Direct elasticities range from {fare.loc[ORDER[:6],'Mean'].min():.3f} to {fare.loc[ORDER[:6],'Mean'].max():.3f}, with different concentrations of near-zero and strongly negative responses. Their BASE- and CF-choice projections do not automatically occupy the centers of those distributions. The estimates and the directly evaluated probability responses therefore remain distinct descriptions of each fitted system, even though they originate from the same model outputs.

For both Qwen scales, the BASE elasticity estimate becomes closer to the human coefficient-based reference after SFT. The 2B estimate changes from $-1.413$ to $-0.682$ and the 9B estimate from $-4.422$ to $-0.685$, compared with H-MNL's $-0.822$. Mean arc elasticity also moves closer to its human reference of $-0.830$, becoming more negative for 2B and less negative for 9B. This mean movement does not imply that every distributional feature improves: 2B dispersion increases, whereas 9B dispersion decreases.

The differences between BASE-choice estimates and mean arc elasticities decrease from 1.077 to 0.197 for 2B and from 3.079 to 0.224 for 9B. When fare-counterfactual choices are included in the projection, the corresponding differences decrease from 1.250 to 0.041 and from 2.125 to 0.115. These are reductions in the reported scalar gaps. Figure~\ref{{fig:fig10}} separately shows the location and spread of the case-mean distributions, including variation not captured by the point estimates.''')
    s=before_float(s,'sec:results-vot-correspondence',r'''VOT is examined to assess the time--cost relationship expressed by the agent's fare and travel-time responses. We compare its level and dispersion before and after SFT and examine whether choice-based estimates are close to the survey references and the same agents' Direct distributions. Computability and component signs distinguish a change in the magnitude of a response ratio from an undefined ratio or one based on unexpected directions.

The conventional models show why these comparisons are separate. Their coefficient-based VOT estimates summarize the generated choices under a specified logit, while the Direct boxes retain the signed ratios obtained by changing fare and IVT in the fitted prediction functions. For Qwen 9B ZS, BASE-generated choices give a VOT of 7.984 kKRW/h, reasonably near the survey point estimate of 6.569, yet aggregate Direct VOT is much larger at 27.771. A similar coefficient-based value therefore does not describe the level of the zero-shot agent's actual condition-change response.

After SFT, both Qwen Direct VOT distributions become less dispersed. The 2B interquartile range narrows from 5.726--29.574 to approximately 0.000--12.863 kKRW/h; the 9B range narrows from 11.672--50.599 to 2.989--13.029. The medians approach the human Direct median from different sides: 2B SFT is slightly below it and 9B SFT slightly above it. The upper tails also contract, particularly for 9B. These changes concern the distribution of computed ratios, rather than proving that every underlying response is economically well directed.

Non-computable cases decrease from 244 to 236 for 2B and from 42 to 33 for 9B, out of 3,468. However, expected-sign rates among computable cases decrease from 85.20\% to 80.91\% and from 92.94\% to 91.12\%, respectively (Table~\ref{tab:tab03}). Fewer undefined ratios and lower dispersion therefore coexist with more component-sign exceptions, especially for 2B.

The scalar correspondence results also differ by scale and representation. For 2B, the BASE-choice-to-Direct gap increases slightly from 2.822 to 3.026 kKRW/h, although the CF-choice-to-Direct gap decreases from 3.390 to 1.358. For 9B, both gaps decrease markedly, from 19.788 to 0.320 and from 8.744 to 0.039. BASE-choice VOT moves closer to the survey point estimate at both scales, but the 9B change is small. These comparisons consequently do not show a reduction in every VOT gap after SFT.

Appendix~\ref{app:representation-check} applies the same service changes and numerical calculation to GC-MNL probabilities and the agents' Direct probabilities. The GC-MNL VOT interquartile ranges remain much narrower than the Direct ranges in both states, even when their centers become closer. The fitted representation and the response distribution thus provide complementary evidence. The implications of these results for alignment and travel-agent development are considered in Section~\ref{sec:discussion}.''')
    s=s.replace('The medians approach the human Direct median from different sides:', 'The SFT medians lie on opposite sides of the human Direct median:')
    s=s.replace(' The implications of these results for alignment and travel-agent development are considered in Section~\\ref{sec:discussion}.','')
    s=s.replace(' Aggregate changes and subgroup agreement are assessed separately in the Discussion.','')
    # The approved Discussion transition remains only after all 4.3 floats.
    (P/'sections_en/04_results.tex').write_text(s,encoding='utf-8')
    print('Results prose updated with preserved headings and floats.')

if __name__=='__main__':main()
